/* book Worked example: Q: A cantilever wall is 4.5 m high from the underside of the base, with a 0.35 m base, base width 2.7 m, toe 0.9 m, and a stem 0.3 m thick at the base tapering to 0.2 m at the top (vertical back face). The backfill is level, γ = 18 kN/m³, φ = 30°, μ = 0.5, qs = 160 kN/m²; concrete weighs 25 kN/m³. Check the stability and find the stem moment.
   1. Ka = (1 − 0.5)/(1 + 0.5) = 1/3. Pa = ½ × (1/3) × 18 × 4.5² = 60.75 kN/m at 1.5 m; MO = 60.75 × 1.5 = 91.12 kN·m/m.
   2. Weights per metre and their lever arms about the toe (heel 1.5 m long, stem height 4.15 m): stem, rectangular part 20.75 kN at 1.100 m = 22.82 kN·m; stem, tapered part 5.19 kN at 0.967 m = 5.01 kN·m; base slab 23.62 kN at 1.350 m = 31.89 kN·m; soil on the heel 112.05 kN at 1.950 m = 218.50 kN·m. Total ΣW = 161.61 kN/m, restoring moment MR = 278.23 kN·m/m.
   3. Overturning: 0.9 × 278.2 = 250.4 ≥ 1.4 × 91.1 = 127.6; ratio 1.96. Safe (factor of safety 3.05).
   4. Sliding: 0.9 × 0.5 × 161.6 = 72.7 kN against 1.4 × 60.75 = 85.0 kN. Not safe: friction alone gives a factor of 1.33. A shear key (or a wider heel) must supply about 13.7 kN more.
   5. Bearing: x = (278.2 − 91.1)/161.6 = 1.158 m; e = 1.35 − 1.158 = 0.192 m < B/6 = 0.45 m. pmax = (161.6/2.7)(1 + 6 × 0.192/2.7) = 85.4 kN/m², pmin = 34.3 kN/m². Both within 0 and 160.
   6. Stem: h = 4.15 m, Mu = 1.5 × (1/3) × 18 × 4.15³/6 = 107.2 kN·m/m; d = 300 − 50 − 8 = 242 mm; Ast = 1394 mm²/m: 16 mm bars at 140 mm c/c (1436 mm²/m) on the soil face. Shear Vu = 1.5 × ½ × (1/3) × 18 × 4.15² = 77.5 kN/m, τv = 0.320 < τc = 0.51 N/mm².
   Answer: Overturning and bearing pass (pmax 85 kN/m², e 0.19 m); sliding fails with friction alone and needs a shear key; stem Mu = 107 kN·m/m with 16 mm bars at 140 mm c/c. */
