/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - p is the water pressure in kN/m² at depth h (m) below the water surface; γw is taken as 10 kN/m³ in design (9.81 kN/m³ for fresh water).
   - The pressure diagram on a wall is a triangle, zero at the surface and γwH at the base.
   - Minimum steel in each direction is 0.35 % of the concrete section for deformed bars, reducible to 0.24 % for tanks with no dimension over 15 m.
   - Cut a 1 m high ring at depth h in half.
   - r is the internal radius in m.
   - With a flexible (sliding) joint at the base, the wall carries pure hoop tension; the rings are heaviest at the bottom and can be reduced higher up.
   - A long wall of a rectangular tank, fixed at the base and free at the top, is a vertical cantilever under triangular pressure: M = γwH³/6 per metre at the base, with tension on the water face.
*/
